{
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The SGD 4.00 the rider contests is the ERP pass-through line for the ECP (Fort Rd towards City) gantry, and the rider was made to pay that one crossing twice: once in cash at the gantry and again on the app ledger. The driver does not deny the cash collection - his own filed statement says that if the app also charged the toll, 'refund him the app one' - and the payments ledger shows the app did charge it, a SGD 4.00 erp line. POL-RD-09 opens with the rule that government-set and third-party pass-through charges are settled separately and are 'never counted twice'; a duplicate pass-through line is refunded in full as a standalone billing-integrity correction, and POL-RD-09(4) makes that correction available whether or not a deviation exists. No deviation finding is needed to reach it.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-FARE-00523"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-09"],
      "relies_on": "derived.money.erp_lines.lines[0].amount_sgd",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The toll the rider is contesting is not a charge their own itinerary would ever have produced. The platform's optimal route for this trip is PIE then Nicoll Hwy and carries zero ERP - optimal_route_erp_sgd is 0.0 - while the gantry actually billed, ECP (Fort Rd towards City) at SGD 4.00, is flagged on_optimal_route false. The rider therefore paid a toll incurred by the driver's routing, not by the rider's journey, and that off-route ERP falls to be corrected as a pass-through under POL-RD-09 rather than left on the rider's bill.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-TRIP-00523", "EV-FARE-00523", "EV-GPS-00523"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-09"],
      "relies_on": "derived.money.erp_lines.lines[0].on_optimal_route",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The ECP routing was the driver's election, not a rider instruction. The in-app chat log for this trip carries zero messages and no waypoint_hint, so there is no recorded rider request for the expressway and POL-RD-04's rider-requested-detour exoneration cannot be run at all - on the clause's own terms the corroboration test is undecidable and the clause is unavailable. POL-RD-09(3) withholds an ERP refund only where the detour was rider-caused under POL-RD-04, POL-RD-05 or POL-RD-12; none of those is engaged, so nothing on this record bars correction of the ECP toll.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-CHAT-00523"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-04", "POL-RD-09"],
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The driver cannot explain the ECP routing away as a closure-forced detour. No traffic advisory covers this trip at all: EV-ADVIS-00523-01 is MISSING, carries weight 0.0 and fired the issue MISSING_ARTIFACT - I disclose that impairment - and its absence leaves POL-RD-03 with no advisory to name an affected road, no validity window and no shortest-lawful-alternative length to compute a cap from. With no closure, no advisory and no rider instruction, the ECP route and the toll it attracted are simply the driver's own choice, and the absence of the advisory is itself citable.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-ADVIS-00523-01"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-03"],
      "asserted_weight": 0.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The gantry crossing itself is real, which is exactly why the app line is the duplicate and not the legitimate charge. The driver's own trace carries a fix 0.0 m from the billed gantry at 15:43:10, so the vehicle passed directly through it and the rider does not dispute that the toll was incurred. What the rider disputes is paying it twice for one crossing - cash at the gantry plus the SGD 4.00 app line - and that single duplicated line is what POL-RD-09 corrects.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-GPS-00523", "EV-FARE-00523"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-09"],
      "relies_on": "derived.erp_gantry_proximity.lines[0].nearest_fix.distance_m",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "I do not overreach on distance, and the rider's case does not need it. The trace excess is 1.33 km, or 6.72%, which is below POL-RD-01's 20% establishment threshold, so no route deviation under POL-RD-01 is established and none is claimed here. The contested SGD 4.00 is the twice-charged ERP line, and that line is corrected under POL-RD-09 on its own footing, independently of any deviation finding.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-GPS-00523"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-01"],
      "relies_on": "derived.deviation.excess_pct",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The rider is not a serial deviation claimant, so no extra corroboration is owed. rider_profile.deviation_claims_60d is 0, which means the POL-RD-08 uplift that would demand corroboration beyond the gps_trace for a rider with four or more unsubstantiated claims is not engaged. I cite that field only for the POL-RD-08 test that names it, as POL-GEN-01 requires; it is used to show no additional burden attaches, never as a substitute for evidence about this trip.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-RPROF-00523"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-08", "POL-GEN-01"],
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "FULL_REFUND",
    "amount_sgd": 4.0,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.85,
    "explanation": "To the rider and the driver — the route is not the issue either of you thinks it is, and the toll is. On the route: the governing measurement is the GPS trace (EV-GPS-00523) at 21.13 km against the platform's 19.8 km optimal, an excess of 1.33 km or 6.7%. POL-RD-01 requires the excess to clear BOTH 20% and 0.8 km. The 0.8 km limb passes; the 20% limb fails, so no deviation is established and no distance-and-time refund is available. The driver's case is accepted on that point, and the trace itself is unimpaired (181 fixes at a 10-second cadence, 100% coverage, no gaps), so nothing turns on POL-RD-07. The two pass-through lines the driver defends are also real: the trace carries a fix on the ECP (Fort Rd towards City) gantry at 15:43:10, so the vehicle crossed it and the SGD 4.00 ERP line is not a phantom charge, and the SGD 6.00 airport surcharge is properly chargeable because the trip originated at Changi Airport Terminal 3. Neither is a billing-integrity defect under POL-RD-09(4). What the driver cannot defend is the duplication. The fare breakdown (EV-FARE-00523) shows the app charged SGD 4.00 for that gantry, and the driver's own filed statement admits he collected cash for the same toll ('I told him about the cash...') and says that if the app also charged it, 'refund him the app one.' A government pass-through cannot be counted twice: once the toll was paid in cash at the gantry, the ledger line for the same crossing is duplicative and is corrected as a standalone pass-through under POL-RD-09, available whether or not a deviation exists, so the failure of the deviation claim does not touch it. Figures used: ERP line charged SGD 4.00; refund SGD 4.00, which is the whole contested amount (charged total on the trip was SGD 38.83). The empty chat log (EV-CHAT-00523, zero messages) means no rider-requested detour could be run and no rider instruction exists; the missing traffic advisory (EV-ADVIS-00523-01, weight 0.0) means no closure defence exists; neither is needed and neither changes the toll correction. The driver profile shows no repeat-deviation pattern (deviation_flags_90d 0 across 250 trips in 90 days, and the deviation is not material), so no POL-RD-06 conduct referral arises. Confidence 0.85: the duplicate-payment fact is carried by the fare breakdown (weight 1.0) and by the driver's admission against his own interest; no impaired artifact carries it, and no escalation is triggered. No referral is ordered: POL-RD-09(4) produces one only for a gantry the trace never passes or a surcharge wrongly applied, and neither is present here."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}

{
  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "FULL_REFUND",
    "amount_sgd": 4.00,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.85,
    "explanation": "To both parties, here is the ruling.\n\n1) The route complaint does not establish a deviation. POL-RD-01 requires the excess over the optimal route to beat BOTH 20% and 0.8 km. Measured on the governing GPS figure (EV-GPS-00523, trace_distance_km 21.13 km) against the platform optimal (EV-TRIP-00523, optimal_distance_km 19.8 km), the excess is 1.33 km — over the absolute limb but only 6.72% of optimal, under the 20% limb. The percentage test fails, so no deviation exists and no distance-based refund is due however the trip is characterised. The driver is right that the trace figure governs, not the rounded 21.1 km on the trip record, and the trace itself is clean (181 fixes, 10 s cadence, 100% coverage, max_gap_sec 0.0), so POL-RD-07 is not engaged.\n\n2) The SGD 4.00 toll was paid twice and is refunded. The ECP (Fort Rd towards City) gantry is a real crossing — the trace passes it at a nearest-fix distance of 0.0 m at 15:43:10 — so the app line is not a phantom charge, and the SGD 6.00 airport surcharge is properly chargeable because the trip originated at Changi Terminal 3. But the driver also took SGD 4.00 in cash for that same crossing, and his own statement concedes it and asks that the app charge be the one refunded ('If the app also charged, refund him the app one'). POL-RD-09 does not permit a government-set pass-through to be counted twice against the rider, so the SGD 4.00 ERP line on the ledger (EV-FARE-00523) is reversed as a standalone pass-through correction. Charged_total was SGD 38.83; the rider ends up paying the SGD 38.83 the trip properly cost, and the contested SGD 4.00 comes back to him.\n\n3) The driver's request for NO_ACTION is declined for the reason in (2): the duplicate cash collection is a pass-through counted twice, correctable under POL-RD-09 whether or not a deviation exists. There is no repeat-deviation pattern — deviation_flags_90d is 0 over 250 completed trips and the excess is far below the materiality gate — so no POL-RD-06 conduct referral arises; and no POL-RD-09(4) billing-integrity referral fires either, because the gantry that was billed was actually crossed. I have not used prior_deviation_flags or any other context-only profile field (POL-GEN-01).\n\n4) The rider's alternative 'off-route ERP' theory is not accepted: no traffic advisory covers this trip (EV-ADVIS-00523-01 is MISSING, weight 0.0, issue MISSING_ARTIFACT), so POL-RD-03 cannot exonerate anyone, and no deviation was established in any event. The empty chat log (EV-CHAT-00523, zero messages) means no rider-requested-detour exoneration can be run in either direction, and no extra corroboration is owed to the rider (deviation_claims_60d 0).\n\nConfidence 0.85, above the 0.70 floor. The deciding fact — that the toll was also collected in cash — is carried by the driver's statement, which carries no weight multiplier and so does not lower the confidence cap, together with the full-weight fare ledger. No escalation and no referral is required."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}
